---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReportCard from '../components/ReportCard.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How ScamPrep Works — Safe Practice Scams + Coaching"
  description="Realistic practice scams by email and text, 30-second coaching the moment they slip, and a quarterly Resilience Report. See exactly how a scam fire drill works."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h1>Practice, coaching, proof, repeat.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A fire drill doesn't lecture you about fire. It builds the reflex. Here's exactly what
        happens after a family enrolls.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <!-- One connected timeline: each step shown with its artifact (all
           simulated, per the brand rule) instead of a card grid. -->
      <ol class="drill-line">
        <li>
          <div class="drill-line__body">
            <div>
              <h3>Enroll with consent.</h3>
              <p>
                You sign up and your parent opts in — always. Tell us a bit about them (email, phone,
                the scams they've seen before). Setup takes ten minutes, and there's nothing for them
                to install.
              </p>
            </div>
            <aside class="mock" aria-label="Simulated enrollment confirmation">
              <p class="mock__tag">Simulated</p>
              <div class="mock__card mock__card--consent">
                <span class="blip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <p>Margaret accepted her invitation — email drills begin this week.</p>
              </div>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="drill-line__body">
            <div>
              <h3>We run safe, realistic drills.</h3>
              <p>
                On an unpredictable schedule, we send practice versions of the exact scams hitting
                older adults right now, by email, and by text and phone with their written consent.
                Drills are modeled on the latest tactics reported to the FBI and FTC.
              </p>
            </div>
            <aside class="mock" aria-label="Simulated drill email">
              <p class="mock__tag">Simulated drill email</p>
              <div class="mock__card">
                <p class="mock__meta">Cedar Bank Security · alerts@cedarbank-verify.co</p>
                <p class="mock__subject">Unusual sign-in detected — verify within 24 hours</p>
                <span class="mock__btn" aria-hidden="true">Verify my account</span>
              </div>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="drill-line__body">
            <div>
              <h3>Coaching in the moment they slip.</h3>
              <p>
                If your dad clicks, nothing bad happens. A friendly page opens instead — a specific,
                usable lesson in thirty seconds. No lecture, no shame. Research shows this exact
                moment is when the lesson sticks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <aside class="mock" aria-label="Simulated coaching screen">
              <p class="mock__tag">The coaching moment — simulated</p>
              <div class="mock__card mock__card--coach">
                <p class="mock__coach-head"><span class="blip blip--alert" aria-hidden="true"></span>This was a ScamPrep practice drill.</p>
                <p>Real banks never ask you to “verify” through an email link. Here are the three tells you can check in five seconds…</p>
              </div>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="drill-line__body">
            <div>
              <h3>You get the Resilience Report.</h3>
              <p>
                Every quarter: which drills we ran, what they caught, what they missed, and their
                resilience trend over time. Proof they're getting sharper, and an early heads-up if
                their judgment starts to slip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <aside class="mock" aria-label="Illustrative report excerpt">
              <p class="mock__tag">Illustrative report excerpt</p>
              <div class="mock__card mock__card--report">
                <div class="mock__row">
                  <span class="blip" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  <span>Fake bank “account locked” email</span>
                  <span class="mock__result">reported</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mock__row">
                  <span class="blip blip--alert" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  <span>Fake bank text</span>
                  <span class="mock__result mock__result--alert">coached</span>
                </div>
                <p class="mock__status">Status: <strong>Mostly strong ↑</strong></p>
              </div>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What a drill actually looks like</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The “account locked” email.</strong> Dad gets an email that looks like it's from a
        bank: <em>“Unusual sign-in detected. Verify your account within 24 hours.”</em> If he
        pauses, checks the sender, and deletes or reports it, that's a win on his Resilience Report. If
        he clicks, a calm page opens: “This was a ScamPrep practice drill. Real banks never ask you
        to ‘verify’ through an email link. Here are the three tells you can check in five seconds.”
        Total time: under a minute.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The practice “grandparent” text.</strong> <em>(SMS drills require written consent —
        see <a href="/promise">Our promise</a>.)</em> A text arrives: “Grandma, it's me — I'm in
        trouble, please don't tell Mom. Can you send gift cards?” The moment she replies or clicks,
        the coaching kicks in: “Scammers pretend to be family in a panic. The counter-move: hang up
        and call your grandchild's real number. You'll never regret double-checking.”
      </p>
      <p>
        Every drill is modeled on current tactics reported to the FBI, FTC, and state regulators —
        updated monthly as scams evolve.
      </p>

      <h2>The cadence</h2>
      <p>
        Drills arrive on an unpredictable schedule: typically <strong>4–6 per month</strong>,
        mixed among real mail so the practice is honest. Unpredictability is the point: real scams
        don't make appointments. Frequency adapts to your parent: more reinforcement on scam types
        they've missed, lighter touch once instincts are strong.
      </p>

      <h2>The briefings: a quiet drip of education</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the drills, we send very brief heads-ups: the newest scams in circulation and
        the tells to look out for. Each one takes about two minutes to read, and they're spaced
        over time so the warnings stay fresh instead of fading the way a one-time seminar does.
        Because drills and briefings refresh continuously from what's being reported to the FBI
        and FTC, the person you care about hears about new scams while they're still new, often
        before they've hit the local news.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap report-split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What's in the Resilience Report</h2>
        <p>Delivered every three months to up to four family members your parent approves:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Every drill we ran, and how it went — caught, ignored, or coached</li>
          <li>A plain-English resilience status ("strong," "mostly strong") and how it's trending over time</li>
          <li>The scam type we're reinforcing next, and why</li>
          <li>A plain-English briefing on the quarter's most-reported scams</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 style="margin-top: 1.6em;">A gentle early-warning system</h2>
        <p>
          Research from the University of Florida found that performance on simulated scam tests
          tracks with memory and real-world risk. A gradual decline in your parent's resilience
          trend doesn't diagnose anything, but it's the kind of early signal families say they
          wish they'd had. We surface it quietly, privately, and only to the family members your
          parent approved.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div><ReportCard tone="light" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card" style="border-left: 6px solid var(--ink);">
        <h3>Which channels drills use</h3>
        <p><strong>Email:</strong> on from day one (plus paper-mail drills where available).</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Text and phone:</strong> added with their written consent. That includes
          AI-voice “grandparent scam” practice calls, so the scam families fear most gets
          rehearsed safely.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          Text and voice simulations are regulated under federal telemarketing law, and we built
          our consent process to exceed those requirements rather than skirt them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    headline="Ready to run the first drill?"
    body="Join the early-access list — or read the honest answers to the questions every family asks first."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .report-split {
    display: grid;
    gap: 48px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 920px) {
    .report-split { grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 1fr; }
  }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2em; }
  ul li { margin-bottom: 0.5em; }

  /* ---------- Drill timeline: the four steps on one connected rail, each
     with its (simulated) artifact beside it. ---------- */
  .drill-line { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .drill-line li { position: relative; padding: 0 0 48px 40px; }
  .drill-line li:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
  .drill-line li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 5.5px;
    top: 20px;
    bottom: -8px;
    width: 1.5px;
    background: var(--line-strong);
  }
  .drill-line li:last-child::before { display: none; }
  .drill-line li::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 6px;
    width: 13px;
    height: 13px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--amber);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(240, 161, 28, 0.18);
  }
  .drill-line h3 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .drill-line__body { display: grid; gap: 18px 56px; }
  @media (min-width: 880px) {
    .drill-line__body { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 400px); align-items: start; }
  }
  .drill-line__body > div > p { margin: 0; }

  /* Artifact mocks — simulated UI, sized like the report card's interior. */
  .mock { margin: 0; }
  .mock__tag {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gray-500);
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .mock__card {
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 11px;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--gray-700);
  }
  .mock__card p { margin: 0; }
  .mock__card--consent { display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start; }
  .mock__card--consent .blip { margin-top: 5px; }
  .mock__meta { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--gray-500); }
  .mock__subject { font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); margin: 4px 0 12px !important; }
  .mock__btn {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper-bg);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 7px 14px;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
  .mock__card--coach { border: 1.5px solid var(--alert); }
  .mock__coach-head {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    align-items: center;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 6px !important;
  }
  .mock__row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    background: var(--paper-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--sand);
    border-radius: var(--radius-ctrl);
    padding: 7px 11px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .mock__row + .mock__row { margin-top: 6px; }
  .mock__row span:nth-child(2) { flex: 1; }
  .mock__result { font-size: 0.74rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--amber-deep); white-space: nowrap; }
  .mock__result--alert { color: var(--alert); }
  .mock__status { margin: 12px 0 0 !important; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .mock__status strong {
    background: var(--amber-tint);
    color: var(--amber-deep);
    padding: 3px 9px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }
</style>
